{
  "sections": {
    "morehen": {
      "type": "custom-advertorial-kapoor",
      "blocks": {
        "expert": {
          "type": "expert",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/adv-board-morehen.webp",
            "name": "Dr James Morehen · Sports nutritionist, elite athlete performance",
            "quote": "“At this level nobody wins by training harder. They win by recovering faster than the person opposite them.”",
            "note": "<p>The session is the stimulus. The adaptation happens afterwards, and that is where the margin lives.</p>",
            "signature": "",
            "image_position": "left",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "head_ceiling": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "01 · The ceiling",
            "title": "When everyone trains at the limit, training stops being the variable.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_ceiling": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>A professional squad has already spent the obvious gains. The programming is periodised, the sleep is monitored, the nutrition is weighed, and the strength numbers are close to what the body can hold in-season. Two teams meeting on a Saturday have done broadly the same work.</p><p>What separates them is who arrives able to express it. Rugby is a collision sport played weekly for nine months; a boxer in camp trains twice a day for twelve weeks while cutting weight. In both cases the constraint is not how hard you can train. It’s how much of that training you can absorb before the next session is on top of you.</p><p>That’s why the search moved from load to recovery, and from recovery to the cellular machinery underneath it.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "ceiling1": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "Spent already",
            "title": "Training load",
            "text": "<p>Periodised to the week. There is no more headroom without breaking something.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "ceiling2": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "Spent already",
            "title": "Sleep and fuelling",
            "text": "<p>Tracked, protected, weighed. The gains here were banked years ago.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "ceiling3": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "Still open",
            "title": "Cellular capacity",
            "text": "<p>The energy available to convert the session into an adaptation. Nobody optimises it because nothing reports on it.</p>",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "head_mech": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "02 · The mechanism",
            "title": "Recovery is a job the cell has to fund.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_mech": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Adaptation is not passive. Repairing muscle damage, clearing metabolic waste and rebuilding glycogen are all active processes, and every one of them costs energy. Mitochondria produce that energy using NAD⁺, and repair enzymes need the same coenzyme to work at all.</p><p>Two systems draw on one pool, and they run hardest overnight: energy production, which is non-negotiable, and repair, which the body can defer. When supply is short, energy wins. The repair simply doesn’t get funded that night, and the athlete arrives at the next session carrying it.</p><p>Hard training raises the bill. NAD⁺ falls with age. In a thirty-something professional those two lines are moving in opposite directions, which is exactly when the recovery trend goes flat while nothing in the programme has changed.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "mech1": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "4",
            "label": "Step 01",
            "title": "The session",
            "text": "<p>Damage and depletion. The stimulus, not the adaptation.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "mech2": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "4",
            "label": "Step 02",
            "title": "The repair bill",
            "text": "<p>Rebuilding tissue and restoring fuel, all of it costed in energy.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "mech3": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "4",
            "label": "Step 03",
            "title": "The budget",
            "text": "<p>NAD⁺ funds both energy and repair, and it falls with age.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "mech4": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "4",
            "label": "Step 04",
            "title": "The shortfall",
            "text": "<p>Energy is paid first. Repair takes what is left, and you feel it on Tuesday.</p>",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "statement": {
          "type": "statement",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Training harder raises the repair bill. It does nothing about the budget."
          }
        },
        "head_margin": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "03 · The margin",
            "title": "A percent is not small when it compounds across a season.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_margin": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Marginal gains get mocked because a single percent sounds trivial. In practice it isn’t a single percent, and it isn’t applied once. A rugby season is thirty-odd matches and several hundred sessions. A fight camp is twelve weeks of twice-daily work. Recovering marginally better after each one compounds into more quality sessions completed, fewer sessions missed, and a body that arrives at week ten still able to train.</p><p>That’s the honest way to describe what this kind of intervention does. It doesn’t make anyone stronger. It protects the athlete’s ability to keep doing the work that makes them stronger.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "margin1": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "Where it shows first",
            "title": "Session quality",
            "text": "<p>Turning up to the second session of the day with something left.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "margin2": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "Where it shows next",
            "title": "Availability",
            "text": "<p>Sessions completed rather than pulled. Over a season that is the whole game.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "margin3": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "Where it shows in the data",
            "title": "HRV and resting heart rate",
            "text": "<p>Read as a trend across weeks, never as a single morning’s number.</p>",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "head_why": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "04 · Why he worked with MUTO",
            "title": "Doses you can check, on a label you can read.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_why": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>In professional sport a supplement has to clear two bars before anyone considers whether it works: every ingredient declared, and every dose stated. Proprietary blends don’t get through the door.</p><p>MUTO is built around three jobs - produce NAD⁺, protect it from being broken down as fast as it is made, and give the cell what it needs to spend it. Every dose is at the level used in the research it comes from, printed in full, and it’s made in a UK facility to GMP ISO standard with independent testing for purity and potency.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
